/**
 * The sky behind the research page.
 *
 * Every other page in this site is headed by a photograph. This one is
 * not, because there is nothing to photograph: the research stream has
 * published nothing, and a stock circuit board standing in for it was
 * the emptiest thing on the site.
 *
 * So it is drawn: a warp field seen through the window of a ship.
 *
 * ── The colour, on a slow loop
 *
 * Three fields — the crimson astrophage cloud, the emerald gas giant,
 * the teal aurora — cross-faded over 96 seconds with long dissolves,
 * so the sky is a different colour when you look back at it and there
 * is never a moment you could call a change. This used to run off a
 * scroll timeline, which was right when the page was long. The page
 * is one screen now, so there is no scroll left to drive anything and
 * the clock does it instead.
 *
 * ── The motion: a warp field
 *
 * Light trails on one diagonal, sliding along their own axis. The
 * round out-of-focus spheres that were here before are gone, because
 * the reference is a frame shot at speed: every point source in it is
 * smeared into a streak and nothing in it is a circle. Three
 * populations again, for the same reason as before — one look for
 * everything is what makes a generated field look generated:
 *
 *   bloom   long, wide, soft. These carry the colour. Blurred as a
 *           group, not per element, so it is one filter pass and not
 *           twenty-six.
 *   core    thin and bright, laid over the bloom. These give the
 *           field its edges.
 *   spark   short hot dashes. The specks in the reference are
 *           streaked too, so they are short trails and not dots.
 *
 * Every trail is horizontal in its own coordinate space and the whole
 * container is rotated once. That keeps the animation to a
 * single-axis translate, which stays on the compositor, rather than
 * eighty elements each doing rotated maths.
 *
 * The trails are off-white, with only a few degrees of temperature
 * between them. They were gold, violet, magenta and cyan, off the
 * film frame, and at full chroma that is a different website pasted
 * into this one — the site is one off-white ink on black and nothing
 * else. So the view is light and dark now, and the three fields
 * behind it are greys turned a few degrees warm, neutral and cool:
 * enough that the 96-second loop is perceptible, nowhere near enough
 * to be a colour.
 *
 * The bloom population is down from thirty to ten for the same
 * reason. Wide soft trails were what carried the colour; with the
 * colour gone they only make the view hazy, and what is wanted is
 * thin lines.
 *
 * ── The window
 *
 * An observation cupola, on the ISS module's arrangement: one round
 * pane at the centre and six trapezoids on a 60-degree pitch around
 * it, in a heavy structure, on black.
 *
 * It got here in two wrong steps. First a vignette, which is not a
 * window at all — it is a dark edge, and the eye reads it as a
 * photograph with the corners burnt down. Then a single octagonal
 * port, which was a window but a dull one. The cupola works because
 * the structure is the subject: seven separate views of the same
 * thing, with a great deal of machinery between them.
 *
 * There is no mask and no clip-path. The structure is one path filled
 * even-odd — the disc with seven holes in it — laid over the view, so
 * the holes are the only places the view survives. One shape does the
 * framing and the masking at once.
 *
 * Over the glass: a little reflected sheen, running across the warp's
 * diagonal rather than along it, because a reflection belongs to the
 * pane and not to what is behind it.
 *
 * And a small camera shake on two nested layers at co-prime periods.
 * What does the work there is which parts of it move. The gas and the
 * trails shake; the frame, the sheen and the hull do not, because a
 * window does not drift against the eye looking through it. Shake all
 * of it together and the whole thing collapses back into a wobbly
 * video. The geometry is in globals, under `.port`.
 *
 * Nothing here is data. It is a drawn sky, and the page says in words
 * what it actually has, which is nothing yet.
 *
 * Static markup and CSS throughout: no canvas, no rAF, no client
 * component, no scroll listener. This is real motion, so all of it —
 * the trails and the colour loop — sits behind
 * `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`, and stops dead as a still
 * frame when that is set. The whole layer is aria-hidden.
 */

type Wash = {
  /** Centre and radius in the 600x400 field space. */
  c: [number, number, number];
  fill: string;
  o: number;
  /** Squash and rotate, for drawn-out masses rather than round ones. */
  t?: string;
};

type Field = {
  id: string;
  /** Painted under the washes, because black alone is not deep teal. */
  ground?: string;
  washes: Wash[];
  /** The fractal-noise layer: colour, strength, and how coarse. */
  structure: { fill: string; o: number; freq: number; seed: number };
  /** What this field throws onto the metal around the panes. */
  spill: string;
};

/** One light trail. */
type Trail = {
  /** Cross-axis position, 0-100, in the rotated frame. */
  y: number;
  /** Length, in vw. */
  l: number;
  /** Thickness, in px. */
  t: number;
  /** Colour. */
  c: string;
  o: number;
  /** Seconds for one traverse. */
  d: number;
  /** Where in that traverse it starts, 0-1. */
  p: number;
};

